1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,024
Why the fuck do we dream,

2
00:00:02,057 --> 00:00:03,605
and why do we forget them?

3
00:00:03,880 --> 00:00:05,846
Dreams aren't random noise.

4
00:00:05,974 --> 00:00:07,605
Your brain is working the night

5
00:00:07,625 --> 00:00:10,054
shift. Here's what scientists think.

6
00:00:10,458 --> 00:00:12,176
Jay sleeps eight hours.

7
00:00:12,297 --> 00:00:13,711
About two of them are spent

8
00:00:13,731 --> 00:00:16,021
dreaming, mostly in REM sleep.

9
00:00:16,341 --> 00:00:17,283
Step one:

10
00:00:17,469 --> 00:00:20,466
replay. His brain replays the day,

11
00:00:20,486 --> 00:00:21,775
sorting what to keep.

12
00:00:22,100 --> 00:00:23,063
Step two:

13
00:00:23,242 --> 00:00:26,426
rehearse. One theory says dreams simulate

14
00:00:26,446 --> 00:00:28,946
threats, so you practice escaping danger

15
00:00:28,966 --> 00:00:30,376
safely. Step three:

16
00:00:30,396 --> 00:00:31,935
delete. In a 2019 mouse study,

17
00:00:32,218 --> 00:00:36,017
special neurons helped the brain forget

18
00:00:36,037 --> 00:00:37,403
during REM.

19
00:00:37,737 --> 00:00:39,696
And here's why dreams vanish.

20
00:00:39,936 --> 00:00:40,832
The hippocampus,

21
00:00:40,853 --> 00:00:42,147
your memory saver,

22
00:00:42,167 --> 00:00:43,429
is one of the last parts

23
00:00:43,450 --> 00:00:44,321
to wake up.

24
00:00:44,597 --> 00:00:45,759
And noradrenaline,

25
00:00:45,779 --> 00:00:47,580
a chemical that helps lock memories

26
00:00:47,600 --> 00:00:49,671
in, runs low during REM.

27
00:00:49,901 --> 00:00:51,331
So Jay wakes up,

28
00:00:51,351 --> 00:00:52,964
and the dream sits in short-term

29
00:00:52,984 --> 00:00:54,871
